=== L2 The Wanderings of Odysseus ===
id: 39efe471-504b-4772-b821-7e2d10d9df92
slug: the-wanderings-of-odysseus
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:28:36.984464+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 22

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Odysseus faces the Cyclops and Circe — cunning, danger and the temptations of the journey (Books 9–10).

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">The Adventures of Book 9: Polyphemus the Cyclops</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">In Book 9, Odysseus narrates one of the most famous episodes in all of ancient literature: the encounter with the Cyclops <dfn class="term" data-def="A one-eyed giant in the Odyssey, son of the god Poseidon, who traps Odysseus and his crew in his cave and begins eating them; he is eventually blinded by Odysseus.">Polyphemus</dfn>. Odysseus and twelve chosen companions enter the enormous cave of Polyphemus &mdash; a giant with a single eye in the middle of his forehead &mdash; in hopes of receiving the gift of xenia, the code of hospitality that any Greek host owed a stranger. Instead, Polyphemus seals the cave with a massive boulder, seizes two of the men and eats them raw. He will do the same each morning and evening for as long as Odysseus remains his prisoner.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Odysseus&rsquo;s first instinct is to stab Polyphemus in his sleep, but he quickly realises that would be fatal: only the giant can move the boulder that blocks the entrance. This moment shows the essential quality that distinguishes Odysseus from other heroes &mdash; his <dfn class="term" data-def="The Greek word for cunning intelligence or craftiness; Odysseus&rsquo;s defining quality, contrasted with the brute strength of other heroes.">metis</dfn>, or cunning intelligence. Brute force would kill him. He must think.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Cover this and recall: what four-step plan does Odysseus use to escape from Polyphemus? Name each step in order.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Odysseus&rsquo;s escape plan has four stages: he finds a wooden stake in the cave, sharpens and hardens its tip in the fire, gets Polyphemus drunk on wine, blinds him by driving the stake into his eye, and then hides his men (and himself) beneath the giant&rsquo;s rams as they leave the cave in the morning &mdash; since Polyphemus runs his hands over the backs of the sheep but does not check their undersides.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">The &lsquo;Nobody&rsquo; Trick</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">When Polyphemus, reeling from the wine, asks Odysseus his name so he can give him a &ldquo;gift&rdquo; of xenia in return, Odysseus tells him his name is &ldquo;Nobody&rdquo; &mdash; <em>Outis</em> in Greek. When Odysseus drives the stake into Polyphemus&rsquo;s eye and the giant screams in agony, the neighbouring Cyclopes come to the entrance of the cave and ask what the matter is. &ldquo;Nobody is killing me,&rdquo; roars Polyphemus. The others conclude he must be ill and go away. The trick is a masterstroke of wordplay and deception: in the oblique grammatical forms Polyphemus uses when reporting his pain, <em>m&#275; tis</em> (no one) becomes indistinguishable from <em>m&#275;tis</em> (cunning intelligence), creating a layered pun that quietly signals the quality that has defeated him.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">However, Odysseus&rsquo;s cunning is shadowed by a fatal flaw: excessive pride. As his ships pull safely away from the island, Odysseus cannot resist taunting Polyphemus and &mdash; crucially &mdash; revealing his real name. He shouts that it was Odysseus, son of Laertes, from Ithaca, who blinded him. Polyphemus calls on his father, the sea-god <dfn class="term" data-def="Greek god of the sea and earthquakes, one of the twelve Olympians; he becomes Odysseus&rsquo;s main divine enemy after Odysseus blinds his son Polyphemus.">Poseidon</dfn>, to curse Odysseus. This moment plants the seed of every subsequent delay in Odysseus&rsquo;s return: Poseidon will make the sea his enemy for the rest of the poem.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Polyphemus as a Symbol of Barbarism</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n8">Polyphemus embodies everything that is the opposite of civilised Greek society. He does not farm the land &mdash; he lives entirely off his flock. He has no concept of the gods or of xenia: when Odysseus invokes Zeus as the protector of guests, Polyphemus dismisses the gods entirely, saying the Cyclopes are stronger than any immortal. He lives alone, with no community or law. He eats his guests rather than feeding them.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Homer signals his barbarism through precise descriptive details. His cave is enormous but messy and unstructured &mdash; milk pails and cheese are stored without order; pens of lambs are organised only by age. He sits among his flocks like a man among animals. In contrast, Odysseus and his men, for all their fear, maintain their civilised behaviour, discussing options and following a plan. The contrast between Odysseus&rsquo;s intelligence and the Cyclops&rsquo;s brute size is the central drama of the episode.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Book 10: The Witch Circe</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">In Book 10, after further adventures, Odysseus&rsquo;s ships arrive at the island of Aeaea, home of the goddess-witch <dfn class="term" data-def="A goddess and sorceress in the Odyssey who lives on the island of Aeaea; she transforms Odysseus&rsquo;s men into pigs, but Odysseus resists her magic and she becomes his host for a year.">Circe</dfn>. Odysseus sends a scouting party &mdash; half his men &mdash; to explore the island. They find a palace in a woodland clearing, surrounded by wolves and lions, which are in fact men Circe has already transformed. Circe invites the men in and serves them a feast laced with a drug. When they eat, she strikes them with her wand and turns them into pigs. Only one man, Eurylochus, escapes because he hangs back, suspicious.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Odysseus sets out alone to rescue his men. On the way, the god <dfn class="term" data-def="Messenger of the gods in Greek mythology; he helps Odysseus by providing him with the herb moly, which counteracts Circe&rsquo;s magic.">Hermes</dfn> meets him in the form of a young man and gives him the herb <em>moly</em> &mdash; a white flower with a black root &mdash; which will counteract Circe&rsquo;s magic. He also instructs Odysseus to rush at Circe with his sword when she tries to transform him. Circe, astonished that her magic has failed, recognises Odysseus and surrenders. She restores his crew, and Odysseus and his men stay on the island for a year, feasting with Circe as their willing host.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n13" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write down the sequence of events in Book 10 from arrival at Aeaea to the restoration of Odysseus&rsquo;s crew.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Hermes gives Odysseus the herb <em>moly</em> to protect him from Circe&rsquo;s magic. When Circe&rsquo;s drug fails to transform Odysseus, she recognises him as the man foretold by Hermes. He forces her to swear an oath not to harm him, and she restores his men. This episode is significant because Circe, unlike Polyphemus, ultimately fulfils the obligations of xenia: she feeds, hosts and eventually helps Odysseus.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Circe: Witch, Host and Goddess &mdash; A Complex Figure</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n14">Circe is one of Homer&rsquo;s most interesting characters. She begins the episode as a dangerous, magical predator who destroys men without warning. But once Odysseus resists her magic and confronts her directly, she transforms into a generous and knowledgeable host. She provides food, rest and, crucially, information: it is Circe who tells Odysseus he must travel to the Underworld to consult the dead prophet Teiresias before he can find his way home.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n15">This transformation from threat to helper reveals something about Odysseus&rsquo;s ability to bring order out of chaos through force of will and intelligence. It also raises the question of how Homer portrays powerful women. Circe has divine power, knowledge and authority that most of the male characters in the poem lack. Yet she ultimately places herself in service to Odysseus&rsquo;s mission. The tension between her power and her submission to Odysseus reflects the poem&rsquo;s broader interest in what makes a man truly dominant.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">The Crew of Odysseus</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">In both episodes, the crew of Odysseus plays a crucial role &mdash; and not always a heroic one. In the Cyclops episode, it is one of Odysseus&rsquo;s men who spots the cheese and lambs in Polyphemus&rsquo;s cave and urges him to take them, rather than leaving as caution demanded. In Book 10, the crew&rsquo;s curiosity and recklessness lead directly to their transformation into pigs. Homer&rsquo;s point is consistent: Odysseus&rsquo;s men lack their leader&rsquo;s self-restraint and <dfn class="term" data-def="The concept of self-control and knowing one&rsquo;s limits in the Homeric world; a quality Odysseus possesses but his crew repeatedly lack.">sophrosyne</dfn> &mdash; the quality of knowing one&rsquo;s limits. The men are brave and loyal, but they cannot resist the temptations placed in their way, and this failure will ultimately destroy them.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n19">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n20">In Book 9, Odysseus escapes Polyphemus through metis (cunning): the Nobody trick, the sharpened stake and hiding under the rams. His boastful revelation of his name to the Cyclops sets Poseidon against him for the rest of the poem.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">In Book 10, Circe transforms Odysseus&rsquo;s men into pigs, but Odysseus &mdash; protected by the herb moly given by Hermes &mdash; resists and forces her to become his host; she ultimately helps him by directing him to the Underworld.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Both episodes contrast Odysseus&rsquo;s disciplined intelligence with the recklessness of his crew, and both test the civilised value of xenia against characters who begin by violating it.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n18">When analysing the Cyclops or Circe episodes, move beyond narrating what happens. The key question examiners are looking for is: what does this episode reveal about Odysseus&rsquo;s character, about the poem&rsquo;s themes, and about what Homer values? For a &lsquo;source analysis&rsquo; question on a passage from Book 9 or 10, pick out specific details &mdash; a word, a phrase, an action &mdash; and say what it suggests about metis, xenia or the contrast between civilised and barbaric behaviour. For essay questions on the wanderings, a common error is to list all the adventures without ever arguing a point; instead, select two or three episodes, compare them, and build an argument.</p>

